vibeos (1:0.1.4.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * VibeOS in German, Spanish, French, Italian, Dutch and Russian, as well
    as English: the phone shell, the lock screen, the launcher, the
    session and every app. The language picked in Settings applies at
    once to what starts from then on, and the session starts in it;
    apps already open change after a restart, which the Language page
    offers. Dates, times, numbers, units and the first day of the week
    follow the language and the Formats region. The shell's font has
    Cyrillic letters.
  * Long words fit: page titles in Settings and App Installer start large
    and go under the top bar as the page scrolls, and a title, a tile's
    name or a label that does not fit is drawn smaller before a word
    breaks or is cut short.
  * Bluetooth pairs from anywhere, not only from Settings' Bluetooth page:
    a car or headset that asks for a PIN, a passkey or a code gets
    Android's pairing dialog. A request turns the screen on, and on a
    locked phone the lock screen says what waits on the unlock; a pairing
    the other device gave up on can be started again from its
    notification for five minutes. A device paired here is trusted and
    reconnects by itself, and Settings lets in only paired devices.
  * A car's hands-free kit gets the phone's calls: it shows the call and
    the caller, rings, and answers and hangs up with its own buttons
    (from the next boot after the upgrade).
  * The status bar shows the Bluetooth icon while a device is connected,
    and nothing for Bluetooth that is only on.
  * Video Player pauses when the car or the headphones it plays to go
    away, as an Android player does.
  * Phone: Call recording in its settings (Record every call, Announce
    recording, Storage location) and a Record button on the in-call
    screen, with the announcement in all seven languages. Recording needs
    the phone's sound card to offer the call's sound, which no supported
    phone does yet, so the switch is shown dimmed with the reason.
  * The assistant is called Vibe. It has a new design, an introduction
    after an install or update, and new things it can do: the torch and
    the phone's switches, a web search, a place's opening hours from
    OpenStreetMap, and a call stays on top while something is asked
    during it. Russian is spoken by tiny-voca-ru, with a male or a female
    voice, and the old Russian voice is removed once the new one is in.
    Voice match is not offered yet.
  * Terminal in the six languages too.
  * The status bar shows the mobile network's type left of the signal
    while the phone's data goes over it: 5G, LTE, H+, H, 3G, E or G.
  * The battery's fill is green above half, yellow from 30 %, orange from
    15 % and red below, charging or not, on the status bar, the shade and
    the lock screen. Power saver shows its plus sign, no longer a yellow
    fill.
  * On a tablet the notification panel ends under Clear all, with round
    corners, and is see-through as on a phone; it grows with its
    notifications up to the screen's bottom and scrolls inside.
  * Apps start whatever the drivers do. Camera, Browser, Dictate and Vibe
    no longer hang at start when GStreamer's start never ends (a camera
    driver that does not answer): the window comes at once and says what
    is missing, and they try again. The Clock's alarm shows its ringing
    screen and the Phone's calls service keeps answering in that case
    too; the sound follows once GStreamer is up.
    tools/start-test/vibeos-start-test starts every app with no network,
    no sound, no camera and a GStreamer that never starts, and checks that
    its window shows within 2 s.
  * Video Player decodes H.264 and HEVC on the device's hardware decoder
    (V4L2), with far less heat and battery; other codecs where the device
    layer says its decoder takes them, and anything the decoder cannot
    take in software as before. Where nothing takes its sound (no output
    but PipeWire's dummy one) it plays on without sound and says "No
    audio output", instead of standing still.
  * One video device that refuses to open no longer silences the whole
    device: WirePlumber's V4L2 monitor is off (from the next boot).
  * Wi-Fi and Bluetooth no longer ask for a password after an automatic
    login on a device whose logind leaves its user record behind:
    vibeos-logind-user-refresh brings it up to date at boot, and does
    nothing where it is current.
  * The shade keeps its blur where the device draws it in time: it
    measures its first openings, and a device too slow for it gets the
    opaque shade instead, decided again after a kernel, Mesa or shell
    update.
  * The notifications scroll as the apps' lists do: they stretch at the
    ends and spring back, and a fling runs into the stretch.
  * Every quick settings tile is a rounded square, on or off, and so are
    the icon squares of Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and Do Not Disturb and the
    buttons beside the brightness and volume sliders. The settings and
    power buttons stay round.
  * GNOME's Files, Console, Software, Calls, Chatty, Calculator,
    Calendar, Snapshot and Weather no longer show next to VibeOS's own
    in Settings, Vibe and "Open with": hidden as GNOME hides an entry,
    made again whenever a package changes the installed apps. They stay
    installed, and Files (Nautilus) still opens folders when asked.
  * Video Player: slide up or down over the picture's right half for the
    volume and over its left half for the brightness, as other players
    do, with a bar showing the level. The volume is the system's, as the
    volume keys set it; the brightness is the shade slider's, and under
    automatic brightness it moves its offset.
  * Settings: the parallax wallpapers' previews are squares.
  * An app installed from Software asks for the microphone the first time
    it records, as it does for the camera and the location: "Allow … to
    use the microphone?". The answer is kept, and Settings changes it;
    a recording refused fails at once instead of waiting. Such an app
    reads which phone it is on (the device tree's model and compatible
    list), and its Open Settings button opens Settings.
  * The on-screen keyboard goes over the app's window, which keeps its
    size: nothing in it is laid out again. Only when the line being typed
    on would be under the keys does the window slide up, just far enough,
    and back down when they close. An app can still be fitted above the
    keys (vibeos.Shell keyboard-fit-apps, which replaces
    keyboard-slide-apps, or X-Vibeos-Keyboard=fit in its desktop file).
  * Navi: standing, or slower than a walk, away from a route, the arrow
    points where the phone points, by its compass (claimed only while
    Navi is on the screen), and in heading-up the map turns with it.
    Moving, or along a route, it follows the car's course as before; a
    phone without a compass keeps GPS's alone.

