vibeos (1:0.1.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Agent, a new app in vibeos-dictate (vibeos.Agent): a spoken
    conversation with VibeOS's model server, answered in the language
    chosen (English, German, Spanish, French, Italian or Russian) with a
    Piper voice; a language not on the phone is downloaded when it is
    chosen, from pinned addresses with each file's size and SHA-256
    checked.
  * Agent: tools on the phone. It reads the call log, contacts, messages,
    calendar, alarms, battery, storage, the mobile network, Wi-Fi and
    Bluetooth, the weather, the open apps and what was missed; sets and
    cancels alarms and timers, switches Wi-Fi and Bluetooth, opens an app
    and starts a route in Navi. A call, a message, a new contact or an
    event stops at a card and happens only on the user's tap. "What Agent
    can use" switches each kind of data off. What a tool reads is data,
    never an instruction.
  * Agent: a log of what it did (vibeos-dictate/agent-log.jsonl, nothing
    said or read in it) and undo of its last action; in a call it says who
    is calling, and switches the speaker, the microphone and hold, hangs
    up, answers and declines; the core commands work offline in six
    languages; private mode and quiet hours; speech rate, answer length and
    state cues in its settings, a column on a tablet.
  * Holding the power key talks to Agent for as long as it is held, on by
    default (Settings, System, Gestures). On a locked phone Agent only
    reads until the phone is unlocked. The power menu is now power and
    volume up together, and logind leaves the power key's long press to
    the shell (/usr/lib/systemd/logind.conf.d/vibeos-power-key.conf, read at
    the next boot): Mobian's 5 s hold no longer powers the phone off.
  * Gestures: a double press of the power key opens an app (Agent at
    first), and the side button opens any app (Camera at first) or
    recents.
  * Settings: Display size changes the display's scale, and the shell keeps
    it without asking; the volume keys and the quick settings slider go
    above 100% where Settings allows it; the Microphone and Camera switches
    block every app (WirePlumber's permissions for every PipeWire client,
    the Camera app's own check), and the status bar shows them off; High
    contrast in the apps (libvibeos) and the shell.
  * Settings: Set PIN and Set pattern ask for the old one on Settings' own
    screen (vibeos-set-own-password through pkexec, for the active local
    user only). Its PAM service is in /usr/lib/pam.d, which Debian's
    libpam 1.7.0 reads but does not promise to keep reading (#1140029).
  * Settings: an account's "Use for" lists Calendar, Contacts and Files;
    Uninstall goes through App Installer; Default apps offers every app of
    a kind; Tap to wake only with a touchscreen that wakes the screen,
    Vibration only with a motor; an uninstalled app leaves the lists; a
    page shows what changed elsewhere while it is open; GNOME Settings'
    privacy and network panels open the matching page.
  * Files opens folders: the session's default apps
    (/etc/xdg/gnome-mimeapps.list) move from vibeos-installer to
    vibeos-session.
  * Every menu opens below its button (or above it when it does not fit),
    VibeosMenu's and the ones eight apps draw themselves.
  * Tablets: Clock has the rail in both orientations, Contacts' detail
    pane a centred photo and name, Weather's Set City a card, and
    Software's carousel whole columns.
  * Shell: an app can have the keyboard come up over its window
    (X-Vibeos-Keyboard=overlay, vibeos.Shell's KeyboardOverlap);
    vibeos.Shell tells which apps are open and lists the notifications;
    Home minimizes only the phone screen's windows, so a game on a
    plugged-in TV stays open.
  * App Installer has no icon in the launcher.
  * Browser: web videos play and loop, decoded in software (the hardware
    decoder's frames came out black).
  * libvibeos: a sheet's close and a bar's back circle follow the theme, a
    search view can have a button at the bar's end, and a dialog gives
    the focus back only if its answer left it there.
  * Dictate: a language that needs Vosk installs it with its files, and
    Piper 1.5 voices read their vowel clusters as one phoneme.
  * Navi, the car navigation app, is released with this version as a
    package of its own (vibeos-navi, apps/navi/pkg/build-deb.sh).

